      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "5ab712de-d5b9-598c-a376-34056b4bb9bf",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Songs: Just the Two of Us, Bette Davis Eyes, Morning Train (9 to 5)\n- People: Grover Washington Jr., Bill Withers, William Salter, Ralph MacDonald, Sheena Easton, Kim Carnes, Donna Weiss, Jackie DeShannon\n- Charts: Billboard Hot 100\n\nFacts:\n- Jackie DeShannon recorded \"Bette Davis Eyes\" in 1974.\n- In 1981, \"Just the Two of Us\" was released as an R&B single written by Bill Withers, William Salter, and Ralph MacDonald.\n- In 1981, Grover Washington Jr. and Bill Withers recorded \"Just the Two of Us.\"\n- In 1981, Kim Carnes's version of \"Bette Davis Eyes,\" written by Donna Weiss and Jackie DeShannon, spent nine weeks at No. 1 on the Billboard Hot 100.\n- In 1981, Kim Carnes's version of \"Bette Davis Eyes\" was Billboard's biggest hit of the year.\n- An edited version of \"Just the Two of Us\" reached No. 2 on the Billboard Hot 100 and remained there for three weeks.\n- \"Just the Two of Us\" was kept from No. 1 by \"Morning Train (9 to 5)\" by Sheena Easton and then by \"Bette Davis Eyes\" by Kim Carnes.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "b3bc0993-7d7d-5349-89ef-3de99081da48",
        "text": "Page: Just the Two of Us (Grover Washington Jr. song). Sentence: \"Just the Two of Us\" is a 1981 R&B single written by Bill Withers, William Salter and Ralph MacDonald, which was recorded by Grover Washington Jr. and Bill Withers.\nPage: Just the Two of Us (Grover Washington Jr. song). Sentence: An edited version reached number two on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100, staying there for 3 weeks, behind \"Morning Train (9 to 5)\" by Sheena Easton and \"Bette Davis Eyes\" by Kim Carnes.\nPage: Bette Davis Eyes. Sentence: \"Bette Davis Eyes\" is a song written and composed by Donna Weiss and Jackie DeShannon, and made popular by American singer Kim Carnes.\nPage: Bette Davis Eyes. Sentence: DeShannon recorded it in 1974; Carnes's 1981 version spent nine weeks at No. 1 on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100 and was \"Billboard\"' s biggest hit of 1981.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
